From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gpu_thread: poll Ocarina semaphore waits at 50 us

Port zortos293/Lichii #446's measured wait-path mechanism into the PS5 GPU
thread. A pending semaphore release is guest memory, so it cannot wake the
host thread. Poll for 50 us while a frame-sized wait is in progress, then
fall back to a 1 ms sleep after two seconds for menus and loading screens.

Evidence: Lichii #446 measured Ocarina's Kokiri-village GPU-fence wait at
about 17 ms/frame with 1 ms polls and about 1 ms/frame with 50 us polls;
the source reports about 330 pending waits/s. This is a prediction for PS5,
not a PS5 measurement. The PS5 build already uses the split GPU thread and
its existing 500 us yield window, so only the sleep interval is changed; the
Linux-only timer-slack adjustment from the desktop patch does not apply.

Break risk: the 50 us polling interval consumes more Zen 2 CPU time while a
channel is suspended and can reduce game-thread throughput or battery/power
headroom. If RADV or the PS5 scheduler does not benefit from the shorter
wake, the console should show equal or lower fps and higher GPU-thread CPU
usage; the 2 s fallback preserves long-wait behavior. A wrong wake ordering
would show as a guest hang or GPU-thread watchdog report, so compare fps,
EDEN_PERF_THREAD and watchdog output against the control build.

---
 src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp | 20 ++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp b/src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp
--- a/src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp
@@ -74,18 +74,22 @@
                 }
                 if (!state.queue.TryPop(next)) {
                     // The CPU usually releases the semaphore soon after: poll for half a
-                    // millisecond before sleeping, or every wait also waits out a host sleep
-                    // (title 1 waits ~25 times a second).
+                    // millisecond, then in short sleeps. Ocarina of Time waits here about 330
+                    // times a second and its present waits for the work behind them: Lichii #446
+                    // measured about 17 ms/frame of fence wait with 1 ms polls and about 1 ms with
+                    // 50 us polls in Kokiri village. A channel still waiting after 2 s (menus and
+                    // loading) does not need the tighter poll.
                     Profile::Scope scope{Profile::Zone::PendingSleep};
-                    if (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - last_progress <
-                        std::chrono::microseconds{500}) {
+                    const auto waited = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - last_progress;
+                    if (waited < std::chrono::microseconds{500}) {
                         std::this_thread::yield();
                         continue;
                     }
-                    // Then sleep until the CPU submits more work, polling the semaphores every
-                    // quarter millisecond (a release is a plain guest memory write that wakes
-                    // nothing). A fixed 1 ms sleep let new submissions wait out the rest of it.
-                    if (!state.queue.PopWaitFor(next, std::chrono::microseconds{250})) {
+                    if (waited < std::chrono::seconds{2}) {
+                        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::microseconds{50});
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                    if (!state.queue.PopWaitFor(next, std::chrono::milliseconds{1})) {
                         continue;
                     }
                 }
